# Per-test time limits (seconds) for tests that legitimately run longer than the
# harness default (--timeout / TIMEOUT, 10s). A limit here only ever RAISES the
# default; it applies to the oracle and every shell alike. Each entry says why:
# tests land here because bash itself needs the time (measured unloaded, bash 5.2),
# never to hide a slow or hung curse.
#
# corpus  test          seconds  why
bash      jobs.tests    120      # ~62s of `sleep`s (wait/fg/bg/kill -STOP sequencing)
bash      trap.tests    45       # ~15s: several `sleep`-based signal/trap timings
bash      ifs-posix.tests 30     # ~3.3s of pure CPU (6,800 IFS-splitting checks): the one CPU-bound
                                 # test within 4x of the default; load scaling alone lags (1-min avg)
cases     2536-bg-launch-order.sh 40  # ~3.9s of bash CPU: its busy-work is a quadratic ${x//0/y}, and that
                                 # ~20ms head start per round is what keeps bash's two `&` children in
                                 # order (${x^^} instead made the ORACLE race: "out of order: 24")
cases     2710-printf-huge-width.sh 30 # ~3.3s: bash writes ~3.5 GiB of `*`-width padding (INT_MAX-clamped widths) to /dev/null
cases     2524-wait-killed-jobs-order.sh 40 # ~4-7s unloaded (18.7s of bash seen at load 9) (bash and curse alike): 150 hot rounds each poll `ps`+`awk` several times, so it scales with load (timed out at load 7 on 4 cores, 2026-09-28)
